ing all the way to a  fully registered architect  there's a lot of steps in between  there's a lot of things you need to  learn  so today let's go through that bit one  by one  first of all i sat all my exams here in  the state of western australia  with most architecture courses and the  title architect is protected in  different manners across the entire  world  and there is different regulations in  each state  so some things to what i say here may  differ in your state and they'll more  than likely differ in your country  so take what i say here with a grain of  salt and make sure you do your own  research first  anyway step one to becoming a registered  architect after you've  passed your master's degree is your  logbook and statement of practical  experience  your logbook is issued by the architects  board here in western australia  and it's a very long form that you  basically fill out every single day  after work  at least that's the best way i found to  fill it out if you leave it for too long  it ends up taking way too much of your  time to fill out but basically what the  logbook is  it's a couple pages about you and then  it's a couple pages  about 80 pages of logging your  experience  very similar to how you had to log your  driver's exam and every hour that you  did  so there's 15 performance criteria in  the logbook that you have to attend to  if you're studying under an architect  then it makes it very simple there's  three categories  you can fill in and you can pretty much  do  the 3300 hours that are minimum required  which is approximately two years full  time at 40 hours a week  pretty easily so the three categories  are executive level participant and  observer  they're all pretty self-explanatory as  well so the executive level you've done  everything yourself the participant  level you've participated in the event  and the observer level well obviously  you've just observed what's going on  i personally didn't study under an  architect i went into a building design  firm  with no technical registered architect  there to supervise me  which was i guess a good thing and a bad  thing in my experience  good thing being that i was given the  opportunity to  do every single bit of the practice and  learn  in my own way making sure that i had to  do all the research  find out what was the correct way of  doing it and then putting it as practice  the bad thing is i had to do all that  research i had to learn it all on my own  and there wasn't somebody  truly guiding me step by step i did have  a director that was very  caring and considerate throughout that  whole process and made the process  significantly easier than most of my  peers who have tried  to study under architects so i guess in  the long run and for me personally it  definitely worked out in my favor  but more to the point if you aren't  studying under architect it means you  can only log your hours under the  executive level  it means you have to be the decision  maker you have to do all the work  that doesn't usually happen at the start  so it takes a little bit of time for you  to get into practice  learn what you need to do and then be  able to start logging hours under  executive level what i've already  mentioned is there's 3  300 hours in the logbook as a minimum to  complete part 1.  what i didn't mention is that there's  three parts in the state of western  australia  and in australia in a whole i'm pretty  sure to complete your registration as an  architect  part one is very simple it is the log  book and your statement of practical  experience which i'll get into in a  second  part two is an online exam that is  usually done in a testing facility  and part three is examination by  interview now  full disclosure i passed part one and  part  two on my first go part three i sat last  year and  wasn't successful unfortunately in all  honesty i was  pretty well aware of the fact that i  wasn't going to pass that exam  when i came out of it i was extremely  nervous i wasn't confident  and i didn't know half the answers so  when i received that email  saying unfortunately it didn't pass that  was  it was heartbreaking but at the same  time i knew it was coming  this year it was definitely a different  story we'll keep going with that though  so the first time i finished my logbook  and went to sit part  two i had 3600 hours logged in my  logbook the second time  i had 6 300 hours logged  i worked my butt off the next year to be  able  to actually gain all the knowledge and  learn every single thing  that i needed to know about this exam it  was  the best year of my life because i  learned so much i absorbed so much  knowledge  and i became a much much better  practitioner so in order to go from part  one  to sit part two you have to submit your  logbook to the board  alongside the logbook you have to submit  a statement of practical experience  it's simply a word document of about two  thousand words  if you're studying under an architect or  three thousand if you're not  it is a very simple exercise it is go  through your logbook  look at the jobs that you've done that  are pretty major go through them  list them one by one pick maybe four or  five  of value and importance and then write a  little bit of a brief and a blurb about  them  which relates back to those 15 criteria  that i mentioned previously in the  logbook  upon completing the statement of  practical completion you submit that  your logbook  certified copies of all your documents  and everything else that the forms  require for sitting part two  and of course this doesn't come free it  comes at a price like everything in life  part two of the ape  or the architectural practical exam is  like i mentioned  an online exam at a testing facility now  i'm not allowed to disclose too much  about part two or part three  but what i can tell you is very  important  part two is five scenarios with six  questions on each scenario  and it is 60 minutes in total it is  usually sat in an enclosed testing  facility  supervised by highly experienced people  that way  they ensure that you can't cheat and  you're only looking at your own work  this year i think it was a little bit  different they may have said it  online because of covert i didn't see it  my part two this year i sat at last year  so i'm not actually aware of how they  did it this year there is  that potential of doing it online at  your own home but i'm guessing moving  forward once we continue to progress  out of covert we will definitely be  going back to those isolated  locked down testing facilities part two  is predominantly on  your knowledge of running a practice and  running a project  it is literally that simple if you can  understand the fundamentals of starting  a business  as an architect and an architectural  firm and understand the process  of managing a job from design  development all the way  to practical completion and project  handover is very important  the main things they do really really  hammer on  is project management because from the  industry's point of view  at least to my understanding that is the  point where most architects get sued and  that is the point  where most of us get a bad name so if  you know your stuff in project  management you're less  likely to end up in that category of bad  eggs  which is exactly what the institute is  looking for good graduates with  lots of experience so make sure you're  highly experienced  in project management and don't spend  all your time just sketching and drawing  yes being a good architect is sketching  and drawing but there's a lot more to it  that's actually one of the reasons  why i failed my original part 3 exam  because i didn't have all of those  project management experience and  knowledge originally i went into it i  believe with about 100 hours of  each category required for project  management  100 hours in the grand scheme of things  to understand project management  is nothing there are so many contracts  out there that you have to understand  read through legalities and bits and  pieces that you have  absolutely no idea what they mean until  they come up in real life  and you have to figure it out so make  sure that whatever firm you go to  starting out  or whatever firm you're in now if you're  going to sit your practical exam  get as much practical knowledge and  project management experience as  absolutely possible if this isn't an  option like it wasn't really an option  for me at the start  i invested in the powers program which  is run by the institute of architects  it is a pretty expensive course in my  personal opinion it's about  1200 from memory once you buy all the  bits and pieces that you have to to get  into it  but it is very very very valuable  from memory it's about 900 pages worth  of information  and about 80 hours of video content they  have  recently as of this year actually just  updated the powers program and the  course  so there is so much up-to-date  information that is definitely worth  knowing and reading  even if you're a completely educated  architect practicing  day in day out i do personally recommend  going back  and learning this information if you  have the spare time  because it is simply valuable to refresh  your knowledge  there's things i knew but completely  forgot so  again it is a very valuable resource if  you have  the financial means to afford this  course i'll leave a link for it down in  the description below  again i have no affiliation to this  course it's not like something i can  sell  it is run by the institute themselves  there's no promotions there's no schemes  it's literally  just a really really useful course for  you guys to do  if you need that added help now apa part  three  is the final examination by interview  it's simply conducted  as an interview it's yourself and two  highly experienced practitioners in  front of you  it is about a 45 minute to one hour  interview  where they literally just drill you with  questions  non-stop it isn't about really picking  at your flaws but it is  actually about picking up your flaws  what the interviewers are looking for on  the other side  is what don't you know where do you fall  short  and is that a problem for the institute  and the architect's protected title  if you know your stuff don't stress  you'll go through it  like a breeze when i went through it the  second time i came out extremely  confident i was like  i knew every question i was very  confident but at the same time because i  had that disappointment last year  i kept thinking to myself don't get too  hyped up  don't get too worked up and if you don't  pass it's still okay  you have another chance even though i  knew deep deep down that i did really  well  so what i'm trying to get across is know  your stuff  learn as much as you possibly can and if  you're going into that interview  prepared you should have no issues  if you're going under prepared and  haven't actually done  council work development applications  worked with high-end multi-residential  low-end multi-residential commercial  bits and pieces learn on the bits that  you aren't sure about because they're  probably going to ask some questions  about it  but overall the interview is very  similar to the apa part two  it's testing your knowledge on starting  a firm and it's testing your knowledge  on  the whole project experience as if i  were a new client coming to you  from day one to hand over so make sure  you go through and learn every detail  between a and z  because there's a good chance they'll  ask any question in between the spectrum  it isn't really something that you can  study for in just  book smarts you definitely need the  experience as well what happens once  you're registered  once you get that glorious email that i  received the other day  well from there on in it's pretty simple  you fill out some forms  you submit to the board for application  they review your application  hopefully they approve it you become a  registered architect and then every year  you have to complete a minimum of 20  continuous professional development  points the simplest way to explain that  is  one hour of professional development  equals one point  so if you go to a seminar that's two  hours long  then you get two points for it they give  you a certificate you keep that  information  and you have to provide it the next year  when you go to re-register  for the following year otherwise in a  nutshell that is basically the process  from day one  to the day you're registered it is an  extremely long process  it took me almost  eight years to complete so from day one  of architecture  five years at university and then  another three years of  actual practical experience which no lie  there are very different worlds  university and real life are very  very different you come out of  university thinking you know everything  you come out of the registration process  thinking oh i know nothing  so it is a very valuable lesson to learn  but  the eight years are extremely valuable  and such an amazing process  anyway that's all for me today guys i'm  completely out of breath  so i'm gonna call it a day if you  enjoyed the video make sure you smash  that like button down below to help this  video with the youtube algorithm  and if you want to see more of my  content smash that subscribe button down  below  hit the little bell if you want to be  notified and as always i'll see you next  monday

